# Generic synthesis of the accelerator with Yosys.
# Run from hw/:   yosys -q synth.ys      (or: make synth, which also writes docs/synthesis_yosys.txt)
#
# Three configurations, because the tables are not all the same kind of thing:
#
#   1. as built   limit/base are register files (they are read at every code
#                 length in the same cycle, so no SRAM macro can implement
#                 them); the symbol table is a real single-port SRAM. This is
#                 the honest area figure and the one the report quotes.
#   2. all-logic  every table flattened into gates and flops.
#
# A third configuration - every table treated as a memory macro - would report a
# much smaller cell count, but only because `stat` does not charge for memory
# cells; it assumes a 20-read-port SRAM, which cannot be built. It is left out
# rather than quoted with a caveat.

# ---- 1. as built ------------------------------------------------------------
read_verilog -DSYNTHESIS -sv rtl/bitreader.sv rtl/huffman_decoder.sv rtl/huffman_accel_top.sv
hierarchy -check -top huffman_accel_top
proc; flatten; opt
memory -nomap                       # the (* mem2reg *) tables become flops here
techmap
abc -g AND,NAND,OR,NOR,XOR,XNOR,ANDNOT,ORNOT,MUX   # generic 2-input gates + MUX
opt_clean
tee -o build/stat_asbuilt.txt stat
tee -o build/ltp.txt ltp -noff     # paths stop at flip-flops: the real combinational depth

# ---- 2. every table flattened into logic ------------------------------------
design -reset
read_verilog -DSYNTHESIS -sv rtl/bitreader.sv rtl/huffman_decoder.sv rtl/huffman_accel_top.sv
hierarchy -check -top huffman_accel_top
proc; flatten; opt
memory_map                          # tables become registers + muxes
opt; techmap
abc -g AND,NAND,OR,NOR,XOR,XNOR,ANDNOT,ORNOT,MUX
opt_clean
tee -o build/stat_flat.txt stat
